          <!-- Film Thickness -->
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden my-8">
            <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-3">
              <p class="text-white font-black text-sm uppercase tracking-widest">Film Thickness and Build</p>
            </div>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 divide-y md:divide-y-0 md:divide-x divide-gray-200">
              <div class="p-6">
                <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-2">Epoxy</p>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">Depends on solids content — water-based is thinner</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">Film thickness in cured epoxy is directly determined by the solids content of the product. A water-based epoxy at 45% solids applied at 10 mils wet will cure to approximately 4.5 mils dry — less than half the wet thickness. This is why water-based epoxy requires multiple coats to build adequate thickness. A 100% solids epoxy applied at 10 mils wet cures to 10 mils dry — a complete representation of the wet application. This mathematical relationship between solids content and film thickness is why solids content is the most important specification to examine when comparing epoxy products.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-6">
                <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-2">Polyaspartic</p>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 text-lg mb-2">Very high build in a single coat — 100% solids always</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">Polyaspartic coatings are 100% solids by chemistry — there is no water or solvent to evaporate. Every molecule applied to the floor becomes part of the cured coating. Professional spray-applied polyaspartic topcoats achieve uniform film builds of 6–10 mils in a single pass, which is equivalent to or greater than two coats of water-based epoxy. This high single-coat build is part of why professionally installed polyaspartic systems achieve 15–25 year lifespans — the cured film is simply thicker and more resistant to abrasion wear than thinner coating systems.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="px-6 py-3 bg-blue-50 border-t border-gray-200">
              <p class="text-sm font-bold text-blue-900">Winner: Polyaspartic — inherently 100% solids, achieving maximum build per coat without compromise</p>
            </div>
