        <!-- CTA Block -->
        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Mistakes When Choosing Between Epoxy and Polyaspartic
          </h2>

          <p>
            These two coatings are often presented as interchangeable premium options. They cure differently, cost differently, and suit different garages.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Polyaspartic for a DIY Install</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polyaspartic cures in as little as thirty minutes, which is what makes a one-day professional install possible and what makes it very hard to work with alone. Epoxy's longer pot life gives a DIY installer room to fix mistakes. Fast cure is an advantage for a crew and a liability for a first-timer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring UV Exposure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard epoxy ambers under ultraviolet light, so a garage that gets direct sun through an open door will yellow unevenly over a few years — most visibly on white and light grey floors. Polyaspartic is UV-stable. If the door is open a lot, that difference matters more than the price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Polyaspartic Removes the Need for Preparation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Faster cure does not mean better adhesion to an unprepared slab. Both systems need the concrete profiled by grinding and tested for moisture. A polyaspartic floor over an etched, damp slab fails the same way an epoxy one does, just sooner and at higher cost.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for Polyaspartic Throughout</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most high-end floors are a hybrid: an epoxy base coat for build and adhesion, with a polyaspartic topcoat for UV stability and abrasion resistance. That combination costs less than an all-polyaspartic system and performs better than an all-epoxy one. Ask for it by name.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The <strong class="text-white">epoxy versus polyaspartic</strong> decision comes down to cure time, UV exposure, and who is doing the work. Epoxy is cheaper, more forgiving to apply, and entirely adequate for a garage that stays shaded. Polyaspartic cures in hours rather than days, resists UV and abrasion better, and costs meaningfully more.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            For most homeowners the best answer is neither alone: an epoxy base coat with a polyaspartic topcoat gets the build and the durability at a sensible price. And whichever system you choose, grind the slab and test it for moisture first — that decides the outcome far more than the resin does.
          </p>
